1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,080
Sarcopenia — if climbing stairs or opening a jar feels harder than before, it's usually not just age.

2
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:10,400
It's often these three habits.

3
00:00:10,720 --> 00:00:20,240
A low-protein breakfast, cardio-only workouts, and swapping meat for soft carbs are the three biggest culprits behind muscle loss.

4
00:00:20,560 --> 00:00:29,120
A typical breakfast of cereal and toast covers only a fraction of the 25 grams of protein your muscles need in one sitting.

5
00:00:29,440 --> 00:00:35,280
Add two eggs, Greek yogurt, or a glass of milk instead of loading up at dinner.

6
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:43,520
Walking protects your heart but barely challenges your muscles, which is why daily walkers can still lose strength.

7
00:00:43,840 --> 00:00:51,200
Add resistance training with bands or dumbbells twice a week to improve grip strength and muscle mass together.

8
00:00:51,520 --> 00:01:01,600
When chewing gets harder, skipping steak is natural, but replacing it with rice or pasta instead of another protein source deepens the gap.

9
00:01:01,920 --> 00:01:08,480
Try ground meat, scrambled eggs, silken tofu, or boneless white fish instead.

10
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:11,680
Sarcopenia isn't just about age.

11
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:21,120
Fix breakfast, add resistance training, and choose protein you can chew — those three habits alone can meaningfully slow muscle loss.
